WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:01.539
Film and Science

00:00:01.539 --> 00:00:04.130
Science has been arguably one of the most
influential forces in modern society. But

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:13.360
it’s a subject that is rarely the focus
of fictional works.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.090
Science Fiction exists as a genre, but the
influence of science is often contained to

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:23.560
constructing the setting for an otherwise
traditional story. Rarely are scientists and

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.470
the scientific process given the spotlight.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:32.610
Often science fiction is examining the outcome
of a specific technology, science applied,

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:37.290
instead of the scientific process involved
with discovering or producing that technology.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:44.170
Black Mirror, modern science fiction-Twilight
Zone, is hardly concerned with the actual

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:50.350
science of what it presents, merely the social
and individual ramifications of those technologies,

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.700
sometimes to the level of caricature.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:56.370
In fact much of how science and scientists
are presented in film involves some level

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:00.910
of caricature. We often see the scientist
as crazed madman [Back to the Future]. Or

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:05.060
science as voluntarily or involuntarily spawning
some monster or hero.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.060
[Spiderman, Captain America, Godzilla]

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.990
Consider the common theme of superheroes or
villain created in a lab by accident. Science

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:15.720
is an out of control force, directed by irresponsible
scientists, and whether what it produces is

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.830
a force for good or evil is a product of chance.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.920
Even when the scientists creation of a hero
is more measured and intentional, [Iron Man]

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.230
that scientist is presented as a self-absorbed,
egomaniac.

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:31.810
Scientist often exist merely to spout exposition
when needed, [Jurassic Park] or as the pawns

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.950
of those who hold the real power.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.700
Even films that seem to give science a closer
examination, they may paying a lot of lip

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:49.840
service to it’s name, but they often end
up casting it aside in favor of other answers

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:58.000
to the story’s central conflict.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:08.849
I’m not saying that all of these films are
bad because they portray science in this way.

00:02:08.849 --> 00:02:15.189
But why is this? Why is the scientific process
so often overlooked in film?

00:02:15.189 --> 00:02:19.409
Aldous Huxley in his essay “Literature and
Science” examines the divide between science

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:20.870
and the literary arts.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.079
Talking about authors he says They have not
been greatly interested in the science as

00:02:25.079 --> 00:02:31.139
a set of logically coherent hypotheses validated
operationally by experiment and dispassionate

00:02:31.139 --> 00:02:35.859
observation. And the in the field of applied
science their concern has been mainly with

00:02:35.859 --> 00:02:41.090
the social and psychological consequences
of advancing technology, very little with

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:43.610
it’s working or it’s underlying technology.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:48.310
Huxley’s words could be describing the tech
film that is fascinated with the interpersonal

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:53.910
drama surrounding the creation of a technology,
more than the the tech itself. But you might

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:59.170
say, “logically coherent hypotheses” and
“dispassionate observation” are not the

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:05.249
grounds for great stories. Stories are about
passion, feeling, and emotion. You might be

00:03:05.249 --> 00:03:06.249
right.

00:03:06.249 --> 00:03:10.250
Huxley goes on to describe how both science
and literature use language to describe things.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:14.069
But it’s the nature of what each needs to
describe that’s different. The language

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:18.590
of science seeks for clarity and purity in
terms of specificity. It seeks to describe

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:23.180
what Huxley calls “public experiences”
or those experiences that are external and

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.230
in that way shared by individuals.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:31.019
The literary artist takes a different approach
focusing on “private experiences”

00:03:31.019 --> 00:03:36.230
“The world with which literature deals is
the world in which human beings are born and

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:41.549
live and finally die; the world in which they
love and hate, in which they experience triumph

00:03:41.549 --> 00:03:44.150
and humiliation, hope and despair…”

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:47.430
The differing goals present a challenge when
presenting science in film. Can those two

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:51.139
come together? Reconciling the two is no easy
task. But it’s one that Huxley thought was

00:03:51.139 --> 00:03:52.139
possible.

00:03:52.139 --> 00:03:56.839
One early step, something that Huxley suggests
to authors in his essay, is to include more

00:03:56.839 --> 00:03:58.799
relevant science within the work itself.

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:03.730
And you can see this happening more and more
frequently. Multiverse theory has been explicitly

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:09.290
influential on certain storytellers. For interstellar,
Christopher Nolan drew heavily on cutting

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:13.589
edge science to produce accurate visualizations
of a black hole.

00:04:13.589 --> 00:04:17.380
But fear might creep into the filmmaker that
the science included in the film will become

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:22.490
outdated, making the film feel irrelevant
in a way it might not if the details were

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:28.260
kept more vague. Or a filmmaker might worry
that those details will be inaccurate, making

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:33.170
the story feel immediately irrelevant to those
who are more scientifically knowledgable.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:37.660
And so filmmakers focus on portraying and
discussing the emotional, philosophical, and

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:42.260
relational fallout that the effects of science
and technology have had and rarely focus on

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:49.110
the science itself. Or sidestep the science
in favor of a pseudo-metaphysical solution.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:53.430
But this doesn’t account for the entire
separation. Huxley’s essay was partially

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:57.890
written in response to a lecture given by
scientist and Novelist C. P. Snow called “The

00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:02.160
Two Cultures” in which he described a cultural
divide between scientists and authors. At

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.320
that time there was little crossover between
the fields of professional artist and scientist.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:12.110
And it makes sense, the level of dedication
needed to be a good scientist and a good artist

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:17.180
are high enough that taking on both is unrealistic
for most. I think the cultural separation

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:22.390
that Snow observed is slowly closing as Huxley
hoped it would, literature now has many good

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:27.830
examples of the gap being bridged, and recently
film has given us some stories where science

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:34.480
itself has played a larger role. Let’s examine
a few of those:

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:41.030
Primer
Primer imagines the creation of a time machine,

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:46.060
as if it were invented in a garage like the
personal computer. Much attention is payed

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:51.390
to the progression of decisions and ideas
that lead to the discovery of the actual machine

00:05:51.390 --> 00:06:04.990
and subsequent tests. It’s a rare moment
when the process of discovery itself takes

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:08.150
center stage ahead of character development.

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:13.160
Primer’s depiction of science can also help
us see that the issue is not always that most

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:17.730
movies don’t accurately depict science,
but that most movies don’t attempt to depict

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.110
the process at all.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:23.420
Regardless of how dubious the science in Primer
might be, the attention it gives to the process

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:27.920
of invention is unique. And the fact that
the writer and director Shane Carruth is an

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:37.460
Engineer-turned Filmmaker, is no surprise.

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.640
The Martian
The Martian might be the film that does the

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:58.780
best job of bridging the divide between science
and film. The movie itself is interested 

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:08.889
in science, much of the science shown in the
film is accurate. Science become the hero

00:07:08.889 --> 00:07:23.770
of the story, the scientist the person who
is able to overcome adversity and triumph.

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:31.930
The Fountain
The Fountain doesn’t depict much science

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:36.300
itself, but it’s an unique film in that
it’s almost completely about a scientist

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:40.370
and the act of science itself. Instead of
turning the scientist into an adventurer,

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:45.580
[Indiana Jones] or presenting spirituality
as something that overcomes science. It uses

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:51.241
those images to show science as a spiritual
endeavor and an adventure on it’s own. Instead

00:07:51.241 --> 00:07:55.431
of showing love as the thing that works when
science has failed, love is the very thing

00:07:55.431 --> 00:07:58.080
that motivates the scientific act.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:01.370
These films show us ways that the divide can
be bridged, and I think there’s plenty of

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:06.389
room for more of this type of exploration.
One response I’ve seen to this discussion

00:08:06.389 --> 00:08:11.330
is that the reason for the lack of depicted
science may have to do with the mundane nature

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:16.639
of the majority of scientific endeavors. But
I don’t buy the theory, filmmakers are great

00:08:16.639 --> 00:08:19.490
at making otherwise boring things appear interesting.
Most of politics is boring and dull, and yet

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:20.490
we’ve often found exciting and entertaining
ways to depict the political process.

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:26.650
I’m not insinuating that science is superior
to things like human ingenuity and love. These

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:32.829
very things are often what fuel the progression
of science. Perhaps the romanticism of art

00:08:32.829 --> 00:08:38.740
works as a check and balance against the threat
of scientific hubris. You can see the less-than-positive

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:45.560
images of the scientist shifting as society’s
fears about what it might produce change.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:52.930
As the ringing the bomb left in culture’s
ears is fading, our focus turns to new concerns.

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:56.740
The dialogue between Film and Science might
be more important than we think. I’ve been

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:02.170
focusing science’s influence on film, but
it goes both ways. Science fiction films make

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:06.829
it into scientific papers from time to time,
and science fiction plays a role in shaping

00:09:06.829 --> 00:09:12.579
the kind of scientific development and technology
that society comes to want and expects.

00:09:12.579 --> 00:09:17.639
Research suggests that cinematic depictions
of space travel helped shaped public opinion

00:09:17.639 --> 00:09:21.850
about it’s viability, helping make it possible
to fund those endeavors.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:27.339
Science isn’t going to become any less influential
in the future, and finding more accurate and

00:09:27.339 --> 00:09:31.800
nuanced ways to tell stories about it certainly
couldn’t hurt… even attempting to tell

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:38.189
those stories at all as has so rarely been
done is a noble endeavor.

